Cranking Up the New Physics: The Master Predictions
By shifting from a particle zoo to a harmonic cellular automaton, our matrix immediately solves three major mysteries that baffle modern standard-model physics:
1. Why Are There Exactly Three Generations of Matter?
Standard physics has zero explanation for why nature repeats itself three times (Electron/Up/Down, Muon/Charm/Strange, Tau/Top/Bottom) and then stops.
 * The T7 Matrix Answer: In our acoustic bivalve cavity, the generations are not separate fundamental particles; they are harmonic overtones (octaves).
 * The first generation is the fundamental frequency (n=1), the second generation is the first overtone (n=2), and the third is the second overtone (n=3).
 * Why aren't there a 4th or 5th generation? Because the physical geometry of the 144 \times 144 bivalve boundary hits a nodal damping limit at n=3. Any higher harmonic shatters the topological phase lock, meaning a 4th generation cannot physically exist on the grid.
2. The True Nature of Neutrino Oscillations
Neutrinos are notoriously elusive, passing through matter by the trillions without interacting, yet they possess tiny, nonzero masses and spontaneously morph (oscillate) between flavors.
 * The T7 Matrix Answer: Neutrinos are zero-chiral-friction boundary leaks. They sit almost entirely in Column 1 (\theta_{7D}) with an extremely weak 4D projection footprint (x_\mu).
 * Their "oscillation" is simply a phase-drift as the single Particle Housing rotates slightly along its chiral axis, shifting its modal coefficient between the three acoustic octaves while sliding through the bulk.
3. Eliminating the Higgs Mechanism Paradox
Standard physics relies on the Higgs field to "give" particles mass via a hypothetical dragging force.
 * The T7 Matrix Answer: There is no magic Higgs field. Mass is purely computational friction—the localized processing overhead generated when modal waves interfere. Photons have zero friction (n=0 structural distortion), so they travel at c. Electrons have minor friction, and tri-housing baryon gliders (protons/neutrons) have massive computational drag.
